{
    "word": "voortrollen",
    "lang_code": "nl",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [],
        "ru-extract.jsonl": [],
        "nl-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "42642f08-526c-3032-8a7b-0796561f632d",
                "word": "voortrollen",
                "pos": "verb",
                "lang_code": "nl",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d3455541-c22b-318a-9fd8-16e5e2fb544f",
                        "entry_id": "42642f08-526c-3032-8a7b-0796561f632d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "rollend voortbewegen"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a60dbea9-8b08-46ef-a3b8-6915fc77c826",
                                "sense_id": "d3455541-c22b-318a-9fd8-16e5e2fb544f",
                                "text": "Tot in detail moest ik me zijn val voorstellen, het om en om voortrollen van zijn lijfje naast de fiets, de bloedbrij in zijn gouden lokken."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "406e8958-6345-3187-9ad7-1e4a51a6d080",
                        "entry_id": "42642f08-526c-3032-8a7b-0796561f632d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "maar door blijven gaan"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "85391f72-18cc-4d4c-a8d7-88473a3a0a64",
                                "sense_id": "406e8958-6345-3187-9ad7-1e4a51a6d080",
                                "text": "Door de steun blijven veel bedrijven overeind, maar het economische nadeel is dat er zombiebedrijven ontstaan, schreef directeur Pieter Hasekamp. \"Ondernemingen zonder toekomstperspectief die overleven op steun en het voortrollen van leningen. Werknemers zitten doorbetaald thuis en dat terwijl andere sectoren soms zitten te springen om mensen en startende ondernemers geen kans krijgen.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "56c2b761-0610-40c2-92b7-579cb9091653",
                        "source_entry_id": "42642f08-526c-3032-8a7b-0796561f632d",
                        "source_sense_id": "d3455541-c22b-318a-9fd8-16e5e2fb544f",
                        "source_sense_description": "rollend voortbewegen",
                        "target_lang_word": "trundle",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "trundle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "68b34c1d-105a-3c5d-a275-b3b1807d683f",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Ellipsis of trundle bed (“a low bed on wheels that can be rolled underneath another bed”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "abbreviation",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "ellipsis"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b7e4df67-1aaa-49c9-9c49-7d9200d7304f",
                                                "sense_id": "68b34c1d-105a-3c5d-a275-b3b1807d683f",
                                                "text": "\"When he comes back will be turned out.\"\n\"But I always knew it was a one-year job.\"\n\"Oh you don't mind being like a rented article from Hertz's, like a trundle bed or a baby's potty?\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2c5dbe11-a785-3243-ad75-663b3ba26561",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A low wagon or cart on small wheels, used to transport things."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fcfb64a2-2c15-4ec4-aeae-92b93d6132f3",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5dbe11-a785-3243-ad75-663b3ba26561",
                                                "text": "[…] you may […] place the whole weighty Clod upon a Trundle to be convey’d, and Replanted where you please,"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "282d5bb3-e3d9-4c50-a388-fe4b2be2f26f",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5dbe11-a785-3243-ad75-663b3ba26561",
                                                "text": "[…] in case the Tree be very great […] you must then have a Gin or Crane, such a one as they have to Load Timber with; and by that you may weigh it out of its place, and place the whole upon a Trundle or Sledge, to convey it to the place you desire; and by the afore-said Engine you may take it off from the Trundle, and set it in its hole at your pleasure."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b758d28d-dd2d-3342-9457-4d770e12576e",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A small wheel or roller."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6b87d7ae-e0f2-395d-aa42-f8ba70351f2a",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A motion as of something moving upon little wheels or rollers; a rolling motion."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "01d1ab4f-c538-4b20-9b97-c25697908cb2",
                                                "sense_id": "6b87d7ae-e0f2-395d-aa42-f8ba70351f2a",
                                                "text": "There was something expert and even vicious in the flick of Paul’s arm and the hard momentary trundle of the [cricket] ball along the curving rails."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "52f42e54-9ebc-36d1-9ec5-a223514cb0d6",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The sound made by an object being moved on wheels."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "521048d9-8de9-4bc0-99c8-820eeec2648a",
                                                "sense_id": "52f42e54-9ebc-36d1-9ec5-a223514cb0d6",
                                                "text": "[…] an old man who could always be located from far away by the sound of a scythe or the trundle of a wheelbarrow."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "84e0bfda-e675-4a80-b278-c004dfbcf196",
                                                "sense_id": "52f42e54-9ebc-36d1-9ec5-a223514cb0d6",
                                                "text": "He could hear the trundle of cart wheels."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ff1d7af3-8223-339c-885e-599c30ee134d",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A lantern wheel, or one of its bars."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "18b26602-8139-4807-b868-b8036ef79196",
                                                "sense_id": "ff1d7af3-8223-339c-885e-599c30ee134d",
                                                "text": "The Cog-wheels in most Wind-Mills are (in the diameter) 8. foot or under […] the trundle is at the least two foot, which is 4. to one."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "451d1b68-b84c-37d7-bc93-6510cfd23483",
                                        "entry_id": "afa6283c-e36b-3b29-9fe7-c1c60b2be783",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A spool or skein of golden thread (chiefly in the arms of the Embroiderers Company, now the Company of Broderers)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "10135364-e24d-4804-b201-3a3976dbedb8",
                                                "sense_id": "451d1b68-b84c-37d7-bc93-6510cfd23483",
                                                "text": "between as many Trundles, Or […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4a282695-b5cd-4ec7-bfc0-4f126f8a22f0",
                                                "sense_id": "451d1b68-b84c-37d7-bc93-6510cfd23483",
                                                "text": "Gules, two broaches in saltire argent, between as many trundles or, on a chief of the second a lion passant gules - EMBROIDERERS' COMPANY at Bristol and Chester."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b85c546d-9329-4ac1-904e-d782c33b9643",
                                                "sense_id": "451d1b68-b84c-37d7-bc93-6510cfd23483",
                                                "text": "Party of six argent and sable, on a fesse gules, between three lions of England, two broches (or embroidery needles) saltirewise between as many trundles or. Crest. - On a heart the Holy Dove displayed, argent, radiated or."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "trundle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "42c6b8c2-2f65-3197-93fd-3b6b44405fb1",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To wheel or roll (an object on wheels), especially by pushing, often slowly or heavily."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4b4d2279-cd38-4f41-96ad-7b314c33193d",
                                                "sense_id": "42c6b8c2-2f65-3197-93fd-3b6b44405fb1",
                                                "text": "Every morning, the vendors trundle their carts out into the market."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bc8ab5b6-4d69-4629-b9db-83d33b619798",
                                                "sense_id": "42c6b8c2-2f65-3197-93fd-3b6b44405fb1",
                                                "text": "to trundle a bed or a gun carriage"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "95f1d3cc-29c3-496e-93ae-ee471c4cd871",
                                                "sense_id": "42c6b8c2-2f65-3197-93fd-3b6b44405fb1",
                                                "text": "When the bin men come down the back alley to trundle our wheelie bins to their truck, the dog becomes hysterical […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d4edca40-5b7c-3910-b5d8-07bc5e81172c",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To transport (something or someone) using an object on wheels, especially one that is pushed."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "09dadb86-d6ce-49a2-9bfa-882697effa50",
                                                "sense_id": "d4edca40-5b7c-3910-b5d8-07bc5e81172c",
                                                "text": "[…] Peter trundled a load of watermelons up the hill in his wheelbarrow."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "de2f467a-716e-48b7-b936-d97a38032254",
                                                "sense_id": "d4edca40-5b7c-3910-b5d8-07bc5e81172c",
                                                "text": "The kraal walls are two feet thick and higher than his head; they are made of flat blue-grey stones, every one of them trundled here by donkey-cart."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "df59cc42-fab8-476f-8822-3794ae804436",
                                                "sense_id": "d4edca40-5b7c-3910-b5d8-07bc5e81172c",
                                                "text": "[…] they are attended like the Lords and Princes of the earth, with mighty retinues, and are carryed in coaches with foure or six horses a peece in them, when a wheele barrow such as they trundle white wine vineger about the towne were a great deale fitter for them […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b3969db9-3b25-49e1-ac9c-f6452f216085",
                                                "sense_id": "d4edca40-5b7c-3910-b5d8-07bc5e81172c",
                                                "text": "1761, George Colman, The Genius, No. 5, 6 August, 1761, in Prose on Several Occasions, London: T. Cadel, 1787, pp. 57-58,\nThe reading female hires her novels from some country circulating library, which consists of about an hundred volumes, or, is trundled from the next market town in a wheelbarrow;"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4d22e7e9-bc94-3593-9517-d49255eedee6",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To move heavily (on wheels)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f374b2c2-cf90-4eb7-86bd-e21a5a801a8c",
                                                "sense_id": "4d22e7e9-bc94-3593-9517-d49255eedee6",
                                                "text": "[…] he can glibly run over Non-sense, as an empty Cart trundles down a Hill."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c42308d0-21a9-46bd-aea5-7c009883a24d",
                                                "sense_id": "4d22e7e9-bc94-3593-9517-d49255eedee6",
                                                "text": "Until the main road from Hatfield to Hertford was diverted a few years ago, heavy lorries trundling through the village sometimes knocked chunks off corner buildings, but now the village has regained much of its former tranquillity."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e8494e6b-3659-42b2-aa31-2ea4248d23a9",
                                                "sense_id": "4d22e7e9-bc94-3593-9517-d49255eedee6",
                                                "text": "Suddenly from around a bend a wagon trundled toward him."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0a189791-d30f-4510-a61e-eb4296d90ec8",
                                                "sense_id": "4d22e7e9-bc94-3593-9517-d49255eedee6",
                                                "text": "My trip ends at Wrexham General. While the '150' trundles the final half-mile down the single line to Wrexham Central, I nip over the footbridge to explore the main part of the station."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8b289028-ebe9-3297-9b7e-4482780f3a96",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To move (something or someone), often heavily or clumsily."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b5f1adb7-a613-47e1-9f65-4d8d03c5f562",
                                                "sense_id": "8b289028-ebe9-3297-9b7e-4482780f3a96",
                                                "text": "I’ll clap a pair of horses to your chaise that shall trundle you off in a twinkling,"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "edd59db3-c04f-4185-9467-ff465ca9e2bd",
                                                "sense_id": "8b289028-ebe9-3297-9b7e-4482780f3a96",
                                                "text": "1928, W. B. Yeats, “Meditations in Time of Civil War,” 6. “The Stare’s Nest by My Window,” in The Tower, London: Macmillan, p. 27,\nLast night they trundled down the road\nThat dead young soldier in his blood:"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "48ad7230-a906-3493-abb3-45ee732046f4",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To move, often heavily or clumsily."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "880a2ed5-5b61-472c-bdc1-a50797e5b2f3",
                                                "sense_id": "48ad7230-a906-3493-abb3-45ee732046f4",
                                                "text": "Betty. They are gone Sir, in great Anger. / Pet[ulant]. Enough, let 'em trundle. Anger helps Complexion, ſaves Paint."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6d6a034c-2a46-4331-9464-7eb78274f0ee",
                                                "sense_id": "48ad7230-a906-3493-abb3-45ee732046f4",
                                                "text": "[…] we set off again, the dog trundling apathetic at his master’s heels,"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c3f8c336-81a4-4925-ac42-508a3011b2b6",
                                                "sense_id": "48ad7230-a906-3493-abb3-45ee732046f4",
                                                "text": "she let the marmalade stay where it was, trundling in blobs down her plump cheeks"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To cause (something) to roll or revolve; to roll (something) along."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e773e414-ab4e-4109-8d4a-a7c10aa19aa9",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "to trundle a hoop or a ball"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "71f16f86-e07d-4a34-af73-0c9c1fd0b84f",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "1565, Andrew Boorde, Merie Tales of the Made Men of Gotam, London: Thomas Colwell, Tale 3,\nHe layde downe hys poake, and tooke the cheeses, and dyd trundle them downe the hyll one after another:"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8bb96a8b-e7fe-494a-8b48-9485330ec442",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "If thou, my Deere, a winner be\nAt trundling of the Ball,\nThe wager thou shalt have, and me,\nAnd my misfortunes all."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b17298b3-ee62-4192-b882-015427907e4c",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "At gaming, perhaps, I may win;\nWith cards I may take the flats in,\nOr trundle false dice, and they’re nick’d:"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2d93b570-97e0-4f47-b90f-df9e2941492b",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "[…] all our dainty terms for fratricide,\nTerms which we trundle smoothly o’er our tongues\nLike mere abstractions,"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5e8717b5-22ca-43a2-8383-45c110146b5d",
                                                "sense_id": "3167808c-421c-3539-96ae-f475fce71b96",
                                                "text": "1818, John Keats, letter to Fanny Keats dated 4 July, 1818, in Sidney Colvin (ed.), Letters of John Keats to His Family and Friends, London: Macmillan, 1891, p. 122,\n[I am] so fatigued that when I am asleep you might sew my nose to my great toe and trundle me round the town like a Hoop without waking me."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "898cbeba-c9af-31eb-b63a-d03ee80efadb",
                                        "entry_id": "eb1bc104-846e-39f8-81a3-70b5d6637d40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To roll or revolve; to roll along."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1152cf49-0181-456a-8d88-e9b1a4300a69",
                                                "sense_id": "898cbeba-c9af-31eb-b63a-d03ee80efadb",
                                                "text": "At Chrystes death, whan the Apostles all\nTheyr mayster dyd leaue, throughe mutabylytie\nMen were founde lyght, and trundlynge as a ball\nIn them was no fayth, but infydelytye"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a79d028-b96d-422b-8a91-8040f7d929d6",
                                                "sense_id": "898cbeba-c9af-31eb-b63a-d03ee80efadb",
                                                "text": "Water is apt to move, being round like Balls,\nNo points to fixe, doth trundle as it falls."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
        ],
